Gnosis stays on production teku-gnosis 0.0.27 (Teku 26.4.0) until the owner does the catch-up: decide QUIC, test in place on the test box, then remove this file.
#
# The first line above is the reason the pipeline shows. While this file exists
# the gnosis variant is not raised by the bump bot, not built or tested by the
# PR checks, not counted by the gate and not published by the release. Boxes
# keep teku-gnosis 0.0.27.
#
# Why: the shared TEKU_VERSION is newer than what Gnosis boxes run, so the next
# teku-gnosis release jumps several Teku versions at once (26.4.0 -> current;
# see the Teku release notes: QUIC on by default from 26.7.0, options removed in
# 26.8.0 and 26.9.0, Java 25 from 26.6.0). That is an owner decision, not
# something a merge should do by itself.
#
# Before removing this file:
#   1. Decide QUIC for Gnosis. Teku now listens for QUIC on 9001/udp and
#      advertises it, but teku-gnosis publishes only 9006 tcp/udp, and on a box
#      that also runs mainnet Teku, host port 9001/udp belongs to the mainnet
#      package. Either turn QUIC off for gnosis in build/startTeku.sh (Teku's
#      hidden option --Xp2p-quic-enabled=false), or give Gnosis its own QUIC
#      port (--p2p-quic-port=<port> plus that port in
#      package_variants/gnosis/dappnode_package.json: a deliberate identity
#      change). The boot test fails while Teku listens on a UDP port the
#      manifest does not publish, so this cannot be missed.
#   2. Test the upgrade in place on the test box: install production
#      teku-gnosis 0.0.27, let it sync, then install the new build over it and
#      check that it keeps its database, settings and validator keys.
#   3. Remove this file in a pull request (with the QUIC change). Its checks
#      then build, boot, upgrade-test and prove gnosis; merging it with
#      "Create a merge commit" publishes teku-gnosis to staging.
